\section{Introduction}
Ezaa (also spelled Ezza or Eza; ISO 639-3 \texttt{eza}) is an Igboid language of Ebonyi State, Nigeria. Search results describe a dialect cluster (Izii, Ikwo, Mgbo) of limited mutual intelligibility, and a close relationship with the separately coded Izi (Izhi) language. Speaker estimates in the sources found conflict (hundreds of thousands to over a million), and all sources found are encyclopedic or student-written, not authoritative. No school materials in Ezaa were found.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks or school programmes in Ezaa were found in the search results.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Ezaa language (Wikipedia overview): \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ezaa_language}
\item Izi language (Wikipedia; closely related language): \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Izi_language}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Ezaa people (Wikipedia; background on the community): \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ezaa_people}
\item ``ezza people'' (a student paper on Academia.edu; unverified quality): \url{https://www.academia.edu/30405742/ezza_people}
\end{itemize}

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
No audio, video, corpus or software resources for Ezaa were found.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Little documentation exists in what was found. Any curriculum would likely need primary fieldwork and community partnership, and could consult Igbo and Izi materials only with care, since they are distinct languages.

\end{document}
