\author{Compiled by Claude Sonnet 5.5 (claude-sonnet-5-5) for Andr\'as Kornai}
\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Fa d’Ambu (Fa d’Amb\^o, Annobonese Creole; ISO 639-3 \texttt{fab}) is a Portuguese-based creole of Annobón island, Equatorial Guinea, with communities on Bioko and the mainland. It is related to the creoles of São Tomé and Príncipe. Speaker estimates range from about 2,500 to 6,600 depending on source. Per a 2021 language snapshot by Agostinho, it is the mother tongue of all Annobónese, with no language planning, education policy or standardization.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers or textbooks were found in the search results; the sources report no use in schools.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Annobonese Creole (Wikipedia): \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Annobonese_Creole}
\item Agostinho (2021), language snapshot, \emph{Language Documentation and Description}: \url{https://www.lddjournal.org/article/id/1262}
\item ``Fa d’Ambô: from past to present'' (2016, \emph{International Journal of the Sociology of Language}): \url{https://www.degruyterbrill.com/document/doi/10.1515/ijsl-2016-0009/pdf}
\item Repository record for the same article: \url{https://oasisbr.ibict.br/vufind/Record/RCAP_e3b38b5d95bacde146ac84a121bfd956}
\item SIL Work Papers (University of North Dakota) item returned for this search; contents not inspected: \url{https://commons.und.edu/sil-work-papers/vol54/iss1/2}
\item CLUL (University of Lisbon) bibliographic reference returned for this search; contents not inspected: \url{https://clul.ulisboa.pt/bibcite/reference/8689}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None found.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Rosetta Project entry for \texttt{fab}: \url{https://rosettapanglossia.longnow.org/index.php?title=Fab}
\end{itemize}

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
The language is vital in the community but unstandardized and absent from education. Curriculum development would need an agreed orthography; the academic articles above are the best starting point. Related São Tomé creoles (Forro, Angolar) are distinct languages.

\end{document}
